d in the low-temperature γ-range during controlled rolling and is used in an attempt to suppress upper bainite formation. However, this suppression becomes difficult when the recrystallized γ-grain size is large. Moderate deformation, when the recrystallized grain size is small, does not permit upper bainite formation and results in fine-grained α [Data: Entities (238, 239, 934); Relationships (348, 349, 1940)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Equation (3) quantifies recrystallized grain size\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Equation (3) expresses recrystallized grain size d_rex as k ε^{-0.5} d0 Z^{-0.06}, where k is a constant, ε is strain, d0 is initial grain size, and Z is the Zener–Hollomon parameter. d_rex decreases rapidly as amount of reduction increases and eventually reaches a limiting value; it depends on initial grain size. Towle and Gladman used stainless steel to show that d_rex can be expressed by Equation (3) [Data: Entities (380, 381, 367, 372, 368); Relationships (618, 621, 651, 606, 609, 617)].\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Equation (3) expresses recrystallized grain size d_rex as k ε^{-0.5} d0 Z^{-0.06}, where k is a constant, ε is strain, d0 is initial grain size, and Z is the Zener–Hollomon parameter. d_rex decreases rapidly as amount of reduction increases and eventually reaches a limiting value; it depends on initial grain size. Towle and Gladman used stainless steel to show that d_rex can be expressed by Equation (3) [Data: Entities (380, 381, 367, 372, 368); Relationships (618, 621, 651, 606, 609, 617)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Processing temperature range and fine-grained α\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Deformation in the intermediate temperature range from 1000° to 900°C refines γ by repeated recrystallization and leads to fine-grained α. This range is equivalent to Range 2, which involves a delay in rolling and is equivalent to type 1B. Fine-grained α can be obtained from fine γ or through a type 2 transformation, and it i